    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-5e",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "5E Serious contraventions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-5e"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.88",
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a\ncontravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory\nthat:\n\n(a) is a serious offence; or\n\n(b) is an offence punishable:\n\n(i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of\nat least 3 years; or\n\n(ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine,\nor a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or\n\n(iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual—\nby a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty\nunits; or\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 5F\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(c) could, if established, render the person committing the\ncontravention liable:\n\n(i) if the contravention were committed by an individual—\nto pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,\nor to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of\n180 penalty units or more; or\n\n(ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an\nindividual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty\nunits or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary\nequivalent of 900 penalty units or more.\n\n(2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or\na contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of\nthis Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that\nkind, as the case may be, that:\n\n(a) has been committed or is being committed; or\n\n(b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been\ncommitted, of being committed or of being likely to be\ncommitted.\n\n(3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty\nunits in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory\nincludes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty\nthat is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to\nthat number of penalty units.\n5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications\nsystem\n\nFor the purposes of this Act (other than Schedule 1), a\ncommunication:\n\n(a) is taken to start passing over a telecommunications system\nwhen it is sent or transmitted by the person sending the\ncommunication; and\n\n(b) is taken to continue to pass over the system until it becomes\naccessible to the intended recipient of the communication.\n\nChapter 1  Introduction",
      "skos:definition": "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a\ncontravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory\nthat:\n\n(a) is a serious offence; or\n\n(b) is an offence punishable:\n\n(i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of\nat least 3 years; or\n\n(ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine,\nor a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or\n\n(iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual—\nby a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty\nunits; or\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 5F\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(c) could, if established, render the person committing the\ncontravention liable:\n\n(i) if the contravention were committed by an individual—\nto pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,\nor to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of\n180 penalty units or more; or\n\n(ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an\nindividual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty\nunits or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary\nequivalent of 900 penalty units or more.\n\n(2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or\na contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of\nthis Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that\nkind, as the case may be, that:\n\n(a) has been committed or is being committed; or\n\n(b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been\ncommitted, of being committed or of being likely to be\ncommitted.\n\n(3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty\nunits in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory\nincludes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty\nthat is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to\nthat number of penalty units.\n5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications\nsystem\n\nFor the purposes of this Act (other than Schedule 1), a\ncommunica",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-5e-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-5e-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-5e-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "88f9f30a964d67c2e441bf24772705e53735bcf6ab8828c5cdce8c620daee705"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-5e",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "5E Serious contraventions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 88,
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purposes of this Act, a serious contravention is a\ncontravention of a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory\nthat:\n\n(a) is a serious offence; or\n\n(b) is an offence punishable:\n\n(i) by imprisonment for a period, or a maximum period, of\nat least 3 years; or\n\n(ii) if the offence is committed by an individual—by a fine,\nor a maximum fine, of at least 180 penalty units; or\n\n(iii) if the offence cannot be committed by an individual—\nby a fine, or a maximum fine, of at least 900 penalty\nunits; or\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 5F\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(c) could, if established, render the person committing the\ncontravention liable:\n\n(i) if the contravention were committed by an individual—\nto pay a pecuniary penalty of 180 penalty units or more,\nor to pay an amount that is the monetary equivalent of\n180 penalty units or more; or\n\n(ii) if the contravention cannot be committed by an\nindividual—to pay a pecuniary penalty of 900 penalty\nunits or more, or to pay an amount that is the monetary\nequivalent of 900 penalty units or more.\n\n(2) Except so far as the contrary intention appears, a contravention, or\na contravention of a particular kind, is taken, for the purposes of\nthis Act, to be a contravention, or to be a contravention of that\nkind, as the case may be, that:\n\n(a) has been committed or is being committed; or\n\n(b) is suspected on reasonable grounds of having been\ncommitted, of being committed or of being likely to be\ncommitted.\n\n(3) To avoid doubt, a reference in this section to a number of penalty\nunits in relation to a contravention of a law of a State or a Territory\nincludes a reference to an amount of a fine or pecuniary penalty\nthat is equivalent, under section 4AA of the Crimes Act 1914, to\nthat number of penalty units.\n5F  When a communication is passing over a telecommunications\nsystem\n\nFor the purposes of this Act (other than Schedule 1), a\ncommunication:\n\n(a) is taken to start passing over a telecommunications system\nwhen it is sent or transmitted by the person sending the\ncommunication; and\n\n(b) is taken to continue to pass over the system until it becomes\naccessible to the intended recipient of the communication.\n\nChapter 1  Introduction"
    },
