    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-6da",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "6DA Nominated ART members",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-6da"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.96",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may, by writing, nominate a person who\nholds one of the following appointments to the Administrative\nReview Tribunal to issue warrants (except to the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission) under Part 2-5:\n\n(a) Deputy President;\n\n(b) senior member;\n\n(c) general member.\n\n(2) Despite subsection (1), the Attorney-General must not nominate a\nperson who holds an appointment as a senior member on a\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 6DB\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nsessional basis or a general member of the Tribunal unless the\nperson:\n\n(a) is enrolled as a legal practitioner of the High Court, of\nanother federal court or of the Supreme Court of a State or of\nthe Australian Capital Territory; and\n\n(b) has been so enrolled for not less than 5 years.\n\n(3) A nomination ceases to have effect if:\n\n(a) the nominated ART member ceases to hold an appointment\nof a kind set out in subsection (1); or\n\n(b) the Attorney-General, by writing, withdraws the nomination.\n\n(4) A nominated ART member has, in performing a function of or\nconnected with, issuing a warrant under Part 2-5, the same\nprotection and immunity as a Justice of the High Court has in\nrelation to proceedings in the High Court.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Attorney-General may, by writing, nominate a person who\nholds one of the following appointments to the Administrative\nReview Tribunal to issue warrants (except to the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission) under Part 2-5:\n\n(a) Deputy President;\n\n(b) senior member;\n\n(c) general member.\n\n(2) Despite subsection (1), the Attorney-General must not nominate a\nperson who holds an appointment as a senior member on a\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 6DB\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nsessional basis or a general member of the Tribunal unless the\nperson:\n\n(a) is enrolled as a legal practitioner of the High Court, of\nanother federal court or of the Supreme Court of a State or of\nthe Australian Capital Territory; and\n\n(b) has been so enrolled for not less than 5 years.\n\n(3) A nomination ceases to have effect if:\n\n(a) the nominated ART member ceases to hold an appointment\nof a kind set out in subsection (1); or\n\n(b) the Attorney-General, by writing, withdraws the nomination.\n\n(4) A nominated ART member has, in performing a function of or\nconnected with, issuing a warrant under Part 2-5, the same\nprotection and immunity as a Justice of the High Court has in\nrelation to proceedings in the High Court.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-6da-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-6da-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-6da-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-6da-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "e0523ada5cbdce84515cb7fb2aafc592344fa7310cd9c0d9e61586bb60fd9f67"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-6da",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "6DA Nominated ART members",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 96,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may, by writing, nominate a person who\nholds one of the following appointments to the Administrative\nReview Tribunal to issue warrants (except to the National\nAnti-Corruption Commission) under Part 2-5:\n\n(a) Deputy President;\n\n(b) senior member;\n\n(c) general member.\n\n(2) Despite subsection (1), the Attorney-General must not nominate a\nperson who holds an appointment as a senior member on a\n\nIntroduction  Chapter 1\nInterpretation  Part 1-2\n\nSection 6DB\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nsessional basis or a general member of the Tribunal unless the\nperson:\n\n(a) is enrolled as a legal practitioner of the High Court, of\nanother federal court or of the Supreme Court of a State or of\nthe Australian Capital Territory; and\n\n(b) has been so enrolled for not less than 5 years.\n\n(3) A nomination ceases to have effect if:\n\n(a) the nominated ART member ceases to hold an appointment\nof a kind set out in subsection (1); or\n\n(b) the Attorney-General, by writing, withdraws the nomination.\n\n(4) A nominated ART member has, in performing a function of or\nconnected with, issuing a warrant under Part 2-5, the same\nprotection and immunity as a Justice of the High Court has in\nrelation to proceedings in the High Court."
    },
