    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-79",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "79 Destruction of restricted records that are not likely to be",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-79"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.248",
      "values:originalText": "required for a permitted purpose\n\n(1) Where:\n\n(a) a restricted record (whether made before or after the\ncommencement of this section) is in the possession of an\nagency (other than an eligible authority of a State in relation\nto which a declaration is in force under section 34); and\n\n(b) the chief officer of the agency is satisfied that the restricted\nrecord is not likely to be required for a permitted purpose in\nrelation to the agency;\nthe chief officer shall cause the restricted record to be destroyed\nforthwith.\n\n(2) In spite of subsection (1), a restricted record must not be destroyed\nunless the agency has received from the Secretary of the\nDepartment written notice that the entry in the General Register\nrelating to the warrant under which the record was obtained has\nbeen inspected by the Minister.\n\n(3) This section does not apply to a restricted record that is a record of\na communication that was intercepted under paragraph 7(2)(aaa).\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nDealing with intercepted information etc.  Part 2-6\n\nSection 79AA\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
      "skos:definition": "required for a permitted purpose\n\n(1) Where:\n\n(a) a restricted record (whether made before or after the\ncommencement of this section) is in the possession of an\nagency (other than an eligible authority of a State in relation\nto which a declaration is in force under section 34); and\n\n(b) the chief officer of the agency is satisfied that the restricted\nrecord is not likely to be required for a permitted purpose in\nrelation to the agency;\nthe chief officer shall cause the restricted record to be destroyed\nforthwith.\n\n(2) In spite of subsection (1), a restricted record must not be destroyed\nunless the agency has received from the Secretary of the\nDepartment written notice that the entry in the General Register\nrelating to the warrant under which the record was obtained has\nbeen inspected by the Minister.\n\n(3) This section does not apply to a restricted record that is a record of\na communication that was intercepted under paragraph 7(2)(aaa).\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nDealing with intercepted information etc.  Part 2-6\n\nSection 79AA\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-79-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-79-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-79-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "17fd26c28a5186533b8e336ff3431b9d4b2ee00d7dd0bb2cf724070dd578e3f8"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-79",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "79 Destruction of restricted records that are not likely to be",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 248,
      "values:originalText": "required for a permitted purpose\n\n(1) Where:\n\n(a) a restricted record (whether made before or after the\ncommencement of this section) is in the possession of an\nagency (other than an eligible authority of a State in relation\nto which a declaration is in force under section 34); and\n\n(b) the chief officer of the agency is satisfied that the restricted\nrecord is not likely to be required for a permitted purpose in\nrelation to the agency;\nthe chief officer shall cause the restricted record to be destroyed\nforthwith.\n\n(2) In spite of subsection (1), a restricted record must not be destroyed\nunless the agency has received from the Secretary of the\nDepartment written notice that the entry in the General Register\nrelating to the warrant under which the record was obtained has\nbeen inspected by the Minister.\n\n(3) This section does not apply to a restricted record that is a record of\na communication that was intercepted under paragraph 7(2)(aaa).\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nDealing with intercepted information etc.  Part 2-6\n\nSection 79AA\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026"
    },
