    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "107A Civil remedies—unlawful interception or communication",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.292",
      "values:originalText": "When section applies\n\n(1) This section applies to an interception of a communication passing\nover a telecommunications system if the interception was in\ncontravention of subsection 7(1).\nAggrieved person\n\n(2) For the purposes of this section, a person is an aggrieved person if,\nand only if:\n\n(a) the person was a party to the communication; or\n\n(b) the communication was made on the person’s behalf.\nInterception—civil court remedy\n\n(3) If a person (in this subsection called the defendant):\n\n(a) so intercepted the communication; or\n\n(b) did an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or (c) in\nrelation to the interception;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the interception by making such\norders against the defendant as the court considers appropriate.\nNote:\nParagraphs 7(1)(b) and (c) deal with the authorisation or enabling of\ninterception etc.\nCommunication—civil court remedy\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) information was obtained by intercepting the communication;\nand\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nCivil remedies  Part 2-10\n\nSection 107A\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a person (in this subsection called the defendant)\ncommunicated the information to another person in\ncontravention of section 63;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the communication of the\ninformation by making such orders against the defendant as the\ncourt considers appropriate.\nInterception—criminal court remedy\n\n(5) If a court convicts a person (in this subsection called the\ndefendant) of an offence against subsection 7(1) constituted by:\n\n(a) the interception; or\n\n(b) the doing of an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or\n(c) in relation to the interception;\nthe court may, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the\naggrieved person remedial relief in respect of the interception by\nmaking such orders against the defendant as the court considers\nappropriate.\nNote:\nParagraphs 7(1)(b) and (c) deal with the authorisation or enabling of\ninterception etc.\nCommunication—criminal court remedy\n\n(6) If:\n\n(a) information was obtained by intercepting the communication;\nand\n\n(b) the information was communicated to a person in\ncontravention of section 63; and\n\n(c) a court convicts a person (in this subsection called the\ndefendant) of an offence against section 63 constituted by\nthe communication of the information;\nthe court may, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the\naggrieved person remedial relief in respect of the communication\nof the information by making such orders against the defendant as\nthe court considers appropriate.\n\nChapter 2  Interception of telecommunications",
      "skos:definition": "When section applies\n\n(1) This section applies to an interception of a communication passing\nover a telecommunications system if the interception was in\ncontravention of subsection 7(1).\nAggrieved person\n\n(2) For the purposes of this section, a person is an aggrieved person if,\nand only if:\n\n(a) the person was a party to the communication; or\n\n(b) the communication was made on the person’s behalf.\nInterception—civil court remedy\n\n(3) If a person (in this subsection called the defendant):\n\n(a) so intercepted the communication; or\n\n(b) did an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or (c) in\nrelation to the interception;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the interception by making such\norders against the defendant as the court considers appropriate.\nNote:\nParagraphs 7(1)(b) and (c) deal with the authorisation or enabling of\ninterception etc.\nCommunication—civil court remedy\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) information was obtained by intercepting the communication;\nand\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nCivil remedies  Part 2-10\n\nSection 107A\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a person (in this subsection called the defendant)\ncommunicated the information to another person in\ncontravention of section 63;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the communication of the\ninformation by making such orders against the defendant as the\ncourt considers appropriate.\nInterception—criminal court remedy\n\n(5) If a court convicts a person (in this subsection called the\ndefendant) of an offence against subsection 7(1) constituted by:\n\n(a) the interception; or\n\n(b) the doing of an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or\n(c) in relation to the interception;\nt",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107a-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "451ecedd0f5c57f52de9c76ab5c0a07fb0d4a4c366cd29cf1de4d1b89b992262"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "107A Civil remedies—unlawful interception or communication",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 292,
      "values:originalText": "When section applies\n\n(1) This section applies to an interception of a communication passing\nover a telecommunications system if the interception was in\ncontravention of subsection 7(1).\nAggrieved person\n\n(2) For the purposes of this section, a person is an aggrieved person if,\nand only if:\n\n(a) the person was a party to the communication; or\n\n(b) the communication was made on the person’s behalf.\nInterception—civil court remedy\n\n(3) If a person (in this subsection called the defendant):\n\n(a) so intercepted the communication; or\n\n(b) did an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or (c) in\nrelation to the interception;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the interception by making such\norders against the defendant as the court considers appropriate.\nNote:\nParagraphs 7(1)(b) and (c) deal with the authorisation or enabling of\ninterception etc.\nCommunication—civil court remedy\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) information was obtained by intercepting the communication;\nand\n\nInterception of telecommunications  Chapter 2\nCivil remedies  Part 2-10\n\nSection 107A\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) a person (in this subsection called the defendant)\ncommunicated the information to another person in\ncontravention of section 63;\nthe Federal Court of Australia or a court of a State or Territory\nmay, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the aggrieved\nperson remedial relief in respect of the communication of the\ninformation by making such orders against the defendant as the\ncourt considers appropriate.\nInterception—criminal court remedy\n\n(5) If a court convicts a person (in this subsection called the\ndefendant) of an offence against subsection 7(1) constituted by:\n\n(a) the interception; or\n\n(b) the doing of an act or thing referred to in paragraph 7(1)(b) or\n(c) in relation to the interception;\nthe court may, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the\naggrieved person remedial relief in respect of the interception by\nmaking such orders against the defendant as the court considers\nappropriate.\nNote:\nParagraphs 7(1)(b) and (c) deal with the authorisation or enabling of\ninterception etc.\nCommunication—criminal court remedy\n\n(6) If:\n\n(a) information was obtained by intercepting the communication;\nand\n\n(b) the information was communicated to a person in\ncontravention of section 63; and\n\n(c) a court convicts a person (in this subsection called the\ndefendant) of an offence against section 63 constituted by\nthe communication of the information;\nthe court may, on the application of an aggrieved person, grant the\naggrieved person remedial relief in respect of the communication\nof the information by making such orders against the defendant as\nthe court considers appropriate.\n\nChapter 2  Interception of telecommunications"
    },
