    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "107G Outline of this Part",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107g"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.297",
      "values:originalText": "This Part establishes a system of preserving certain stored\ncommunications that are held by a carrier. The purpose of the\npreservation is to prevent the communications from being\ndestroyed before they can be accessed under certain warrants\nissued under this Act.\nUnder the system, certain agencies can give a preservation notice\nto a carrier requiring the carrier to preserve all stored\ncommunications that the carrier holds that relate to the person or\ntelecommunications service specified in the notice. The carrier will\nbreach its obligations under section 313 of the Telecommunications\nAct 1997 if it does not comply with the notice.\nThere are 2 types of preservation notices: domestic preservation\nnotices (which cover stored communications that might relate\neither to a contravention of certain Australian laws or to security)\nand foreign preservation notices (which cover stored\ncommunications that might relate to a contravention of certain\nforeign laws or to certain international offences).\nDivision 2 deals with domestic preservation notices. There are 2\nkinds of domestic preservation notices:\n\n(a)\nhistoric domestic preservation notices, which cover\nstored communications held by the carrier on a\nparticular day; and\n\nChapter 3  Preserving and accessing stored communications",
      "skos:definition": "This Part establishes a system of preserving certain stored\ncommunications that are held by a carrier. The purpose of the\npreservation is to prevent the communications from being\ndestroyed before they can be accessed under certain warrants\nissued under this Act.\nUnder the system, certain agencies can give a preservation notice\nto a carrier requiring the carrier to preserve all stored\ncommunications that the carrier holds that relate to the person or\ntelecommunications service specified in the notice. The carrier will\nbreach its obligations under section 313 of the Telecommunications\nAct 1997 if it does not comply with the notice.\nThere are 2 types of preservation notices: domestic preservation\nnotices (which cover stored communications that might relate\neither to a contravention of certain Australian laws or to security)\nand foreign preservation notices (which cover stored\ncommunications that might relate to a contravention of certain\nforeign laws or to certain international offences).\nDivision 2 deals with domestic preservation notices. There are 2\nkinds of domestic preservation notices:\n\n(a)\nhistoric domestic preservation notices, which cover\nstored communications held by the carrier on a\nparticular day; and\n\nChapter 3  Preserving and accessing stored communications",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "ec56b4df84e33cfee70fb8a122774ffa65da77afce7daef1d929846cab15c94d",
      "skos:note": "Carrier must preserve stored communications upon receipt of preservation notice."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107g",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "107G Outline of this Part",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 297,
      "values:originalText": "This Part establishes a system of preserving certain stored\ncommunications that are held by a carrier. The purpose of the\npreservation is to prevent the communications from being\ndestroyed before they can be accessed under certain warrants\nissued under this Act.\nUnder the system, certain agencies can give a preservation notice\nto a carrier requiring the carrier to preserve all stored\ncommunications that the carrier holds that relate to the person or\ntelecommunications service specified in the notice. The carrier will\nbreach its obligations under section 313 of the Telecommunications\nAct 1997 if it does not comply with the notice.\nThere are 2 types of preservation notices: domestic preservation\nnotices (which cover stored communications that might relate\neither to a contravention of certain Australian laws or to security)\nand foreign preservation notices (which cover stored\ncommunications that might relate to a contravention of certain\nforeign laws or to certain international offences).\nDivision 2 deals with domestic preservation notices. There are 2\nkinds of domestic preservation notices:\n\n(a)\nhistoric domestic preservation notices, which cover\nstored communications held by the carrier on a\nparticular day; and\n\nChapter 3  Preserving and accessing stored communications"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107g-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "MUST",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Carrier has a duty to preserve communications.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107h",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "107H Domestic preservation notices",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107h"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.299",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An issuing agency may give a carrier a written notice (a domestic\npreservation notice) requiring the carrier to preserve, while the\nnotice is in force, all stored communications that:\n\n(a) relate to the person or telecommunications service specified\nin the notice; and\n\n(b) the carrier holds at any time during:\n\n(i) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the day the carrier receives\nthe notice (in which case the notice is an historic\ndomestic preservation notice); or\n\n(ii) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the 29th day after the day\nthe carrier receives the notice (in which case the notice\nis an ongoing domestic preservation notice).\n\n(2) However, the agency can only give the notice if the conditions in\nsubsection 107J(1) or (2) are satisfied.\n\n(3) In the notice, the agency can only specify:\n\n(a) one person; or\n\n(b) one or more telecommunications services; or\n\n(c) one person and one or more telecommunications services.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An issuing agency may give a carrier a written notice (a domestic\npreservation notice) requiring the carrier to preserve, while the\nnotice is in force, all stored communications that:\n\n(a) relate to the person or telecommunications service specified\nin the notice; and\n\n(b) the carrier holds at any time during:\n\n(i) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the day the carrier receives\nthe notice (in which case the notice is an historic\ndomestic preservation notice); or\n\n(ii) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the 29th day after the day\nthe carrier receives the notice (in which case the notice\nis an ongoing domestic preservation notice).\n\n(2) However, the agency can only give the notice if the conditions in\nsubsection 107J(1) or (2) are satisfied.\n\n(3) In the notice, the agency can only specify:\n\n(a) one person; or\n\n(b) one or more telecommunications services; or\n\n(c) one person and one or more telecommunications services.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107h-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107h-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-107h-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b88afcabd2c2de58e7901b096226fa139d0b137273cbcc1f009120369dcd5da1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-107h",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "107H Domestic preservation notices",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 299,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An issuing agency may give a carrier a written notice (a domestic\npreservation notice) requiring the carrier to preserve, while the\nnotice is in force, all stored communications that:\n\n(a) relate to the person or telecommunications service specified\nin the notice; and\n\n(b) the carrier holds at any time during:\n\n(i) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the day the carrier receives\nthe notice (in which case the notice is an historic\ndomestic preservation notice); or\n\n(ii) the period that starts at the time the carrier receives the\nnotice and ends at the end of the 29th day after the day\nthe carrier receives the notice (in which case the notice\nis an ongoing domestic preservation notice).\n\n(2) However, the agency can only give the notice if the conditions in\nsubsection 107J(1) or (2) are satisfied.\n\n(3) In the notice, the agency can only specify:\n\n(a) one person; or\n\n(b) one or more telecommunications services; or\n\n(c) one person and one or more telecommunications services."
    },
