    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-130",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "130 Evidentiary certificates relating to actions by criminal",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-130"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.332",
      "values:originalText": "law-enforcement agencies\n\n(1) A certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nissue a written certificate signed by him or her setting out such\nfacts as he or she considers relevant with respect to:\n\n(a) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with the execution of a stored communications\nwarrant; or\n\n(b) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with:\n\n(i) the communication by a person to another person of\ninformation obtained by the execution of such a\nwarrant; or\n\n(ii) the making use of such information; or\n\n(iii) the making of a record of such information; or\n\n(iv) the custody of a record of such information; or\n\n(v) the giving in evidence of such information.\n\nPreserving and accessing stored communications  Chapter 3\nAccess by criminal law-enforcement agencies to stored communications  Part 3-3\nProvisions relating to execution of warrants  Division 4\n\nSection 131\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A document purporting to be a certificate issued under this section\nby a certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency and to\nbe signed by him or her:\n\n(a) is to be received in evidence in an exempt proceeding\nwithout further proof; and\n\n(b) in an exempt proceeding, is prima facie evidence of the\nmatters stated in the document.",
      "skos:definition": "law-enforcement agencies\n\n(1) A certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nissue a written certificate signed by him or her setting out such\nfacts as he or she considers relevant with respect to:\n\n(a) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with the execution of a stored communications\nwarrant; or\n\n(b) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with:\n\n(i) the communication by a person to another person of\ninformation obtained by the execution of such a\nwarrant; or\n\n(ii) the making use of such information; or\n\n(iii) the making of a record of such information; or\n\n(iv) the custody of a record of such information; or\n\n(v) the giving in evidence of such information.\n\nPreserving and accessing stored communications  Chapter 3\nAccess by criminal law-enforcement agencies to stored communications  Part 3-3\nProvisions relating to execution of warrants  Division 4\n\nSection 131\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A document purporting to be a certificate issued under this section\nby a certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency and to\nbe signed by him or her:\n\n(a) is to be received in evidence in an exempt proceeding\nwithout further proof; and\n\n(b) in an exempt proceeding, is prima facie evidence of the\nmatters stated in the document.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-130-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-130-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f85488adc04fb61660b743b0e6cf473ccee1f33c9d577829e7b2cde21bbbd686"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-130",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "130 Evidentiary certificates relating to actions by criminal",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 332,
      "values:originalText": "law-enforcement agencies\n\n(1) A certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nissue a written certificate signed by him or her setting out such\nfacts as he or she considers relevant with respect to:\n\n(a) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with the execution of a stored communications\nwarrant; or\n\n(b) anything done by an officer or staff member of the agency in\nconnection with:\n\n(i) the communication by a person to another person of\ninformation obtained by the execution of such a\nwarrant; or\n\n(ii) the making use of such information; or\n\n(iii) the making of a record of such information; or\n\n(iv) the custody of a record of such information; or\n\n(v) the giving in evidence of such information.\n\nPreserving and accessing stored communications  Chapter 3\nAccess by criminal law-enforcement agencies to stored communications  Part 3-3\nProvisions relating to execution of warrants  Division 4\n\nSection 131\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A document purporting to be a certificate issued under this section\nby a certifying officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency and to\nbe signed by him or her:\n\n(a) is to be received in evidence in an exempt proceeding\nwithout further proof; and\n\n(b) in an exempt proceeding, is prima facie evidence of the\nmatters stated in the document."
    },
