    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-162-ss-3",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "162(3) Report to set out how many applications made and warrants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-162-ss-3"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.356",
      "values:originalText": "(3) An international offence, for an enforcement agency, is:\n\n(a) an offence against a law of a foreign country; or\n\n(b) a crime within the jurisdiction of the ICC; or\n\n(c) a War Crimes Tribunal offence;\nin respect of which a stored communications warrant was issued as\na result of an international assistance application made by the\nagency during the year.\n\nChapter 3  Preserving and accessing stored communications",
      "skos:definition": "(3) An international offence, for an enforcement agency, is:\n\n(a) an offence against a law of a foreign country; or\n\n(b) a crime within the jurisdiction of the ICC; or\n\n(c) a War Crimes Tribunal offence;\nin respect of which a stored communications warrant was issued as\na result of an international assistance application made by the\nagency during the year.\n\nChapter 3  Preserving and accessing stored communications",
