    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-178",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "178 Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-178"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.373",
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcement of the criminal law etc.\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless the\nauthorised officer is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably\nnecessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the enforcement of the criminal law; or\n\n(b) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "skos:definition": "documents—enforcement of the criminal law etc.\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless the\nauthorised officer is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably\nnecessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the enforcement of the criminal law; or\n\n(b) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-178-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-178-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-178-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "32a2b1bfeeb6b583d0b2103e6e011c51d5e49bfdd9081209c75969d4b3d00d0b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-178",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "178 Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 373,
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcement of the criminal law etc.\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless the\nauthorised officer is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably\nnecessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the enforcement of the criminal law; or\n\n(b) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data"
    },
