    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-179",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "179 Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-179"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.374",
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary\npenalty or protection of the public revenue\n\n(1) Sections 276 and 277 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do not\nprevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nEnforcement agencies  Division 4\n\nSection 180\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for the\nenforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary penalty or for the\nprotection of the public revenue.",
      "skos:definition": "documents—enforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary\npenalty or protection of the public revenue\n\n(1) Sections 276 and 277 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do not\nprevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nEnforcement agencies  Division 4\n\nSection 180\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for the\nenforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary penalty or for the\nprotection of the public revenue.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-179-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-179-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-179-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "9047a628e9e5897d53c8f05ba431155641e960815fad6becf394eb5bf8c412d9"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-179",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "179 Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 374,
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary\npenalty or protection of the public revenue\n\n(1) Sections 276 and 277 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do not\nprevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of an enforcement agency may authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nEnforcement agencies  Division 4\n\nSection 180\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for the\nenforcement of a law imposing a pecuniary penalty or for the\nprotection of the public revenue."
    },
