    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "180 Authorisations for access to prospective information or",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.375",
      "values:originalText": "documents\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\nAuthorisation for access to existing information or documents may\nalso be sought\n\n(3) The authorised officer may, in that authorisation, also authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the authorisation comes into\nforce.\nLimits on making the authorisation\n\n(4) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the investigation of a serious offence; or\n\n(b) in any case—the investigation of an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that is punishable\nby imprisonment for at least 3 years; or\n\n(c) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "skos:definition": "documents\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\nAuthorisation for access to existing information or documents may\nalso be sought\n\n(3) The authorised officer may, in that authorisation, also authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the authorisation comes into\nforce.\nLimits on making the authorisation\n\n(4) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the investigation of a serious offence; or\n\n(b) in any case—the investigation of an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that is punishable\nby imprisonment for at least 3 years; or\n\n(c) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "9b16b44d0e0a5cac4f968befb0b5b5eba913f9ef3dfa1501ed728b6ead72a357"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "180 Authorisations for access to prospective information or",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 375,
      "values:originalText": "documents\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of a criminal law-enforcement agency may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\nAuthorisation for access to existing information or documents may\nalso be sought\n\n(3) The authorised officer may, in that authorisation, also authorise the\ndisclosure of specified information or specified documents that\ncame into existence before the time the authorisation comes into\nforce.\nLimits on making the authorisation\n\n(4) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) in any case—the investigation of a serious offence; or\n\n(b) in any case—the investigation of an offence against a law of\nthe Commonwealth, a State or a Territory that is punishable\nby imprisonment for at least 3 years; or\n\n(c) in the case of an authorised officer of the ACC—the\nperformance of the ACC’s function covered by\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data"
    },
