    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "180A Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.377",
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcing foreign or international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that came into existence before the time the person\nfrom whom the disclosure is sought receives notification of the\nauthorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) the enforcement of the criminal law of a foreign country; or\n\n(b) an investigation or prosecution of a crime within the\njurisdiction of the ICC; or\n\n(c) an investigation or prosecution of a War Crimes Tribunal\noffence.\nDisclosure to a foreign law enforcement agency\n\n(4) If specified information or specified documents are disclosed\nbecause of an authorisation given under subsection (2), an\nauthorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may authorise\nthe disclosure of the information or documents so disclosed to a\nforeign law enforcement agency.\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "skos:definition": "documents—enforcing foreign or international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that came into existence before the time the person\nfrom whom the disclosure is sought receives notification of the\nauthorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) the enforcement of the criminal law of a foreign country; or\n\n(b) an investigation or prosecution of a crime within the\njurisdiction of the ICC; or\n\n(c) an investigation or prosecution of a War Crimes Tribunal\noffence.\nDisclosure to a foreign law enforcement agency\n\n(4) If specified information or specified documents are disclosed\nbecause of an authorisation given under subsection (2), an\nauthorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may authorise\nthe disclosure of the information or documents so disclosed to a\nforeign law enforcement agency.\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "47966fb9ca6f643fafa45c9f7cf6e3c05868fd965317f916ac7cfbc3776920e7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "180A Authorisations for access to existing information or",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 377,
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcing foreign or international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2).\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that came into existence before the time the person\nfrom whom the disclosure is sought receives notification of the\nauthorisation.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless he or\nshe is satisfied that the disclosure is reasonably necessary for:\n\n(a) the enforcement of the criminal law of a foreign country; or\n\n(b) an investigation or prosecution of a crime within the\njurisdiction of the ICC; or\n\n(c) an investigation or prosecution of a War Crimes Tribunal\noffence.\nDisclosure to a foreign law enforcement agency\n\n(4) If specified information or specified documents are disclosed\nbecause of an authorisation given under subsection (2), an\nauthorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may authorise\nthe disclosure of the information or documents so disclosed to a\nforeign law enforcement agency.\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data"
    },
