    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180a-ss-4-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Permit",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Authorisation for disclosure of information to foreign law enforcement agency",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(4) If specified information or specified documents are disclosed because of an authorisation given under subsection (2)",
      "cml:conclusionText": "An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may authorise the disclosure of the information or documents so disclosed to a foreign law enforcement agency."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "180B Authorisations for access to prospective information or",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180b"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979.pdf, p.378",
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcing international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2) of this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless:\n\n(a) the Attorney-General has authorised the making of the\nauthorisation under a provision mentioned in an item of the\nfollowing table; and\n\n(b) the authorised officer is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the disclosure is reasonably necessary for an\ninvestigation or proceeding referred to in that table item;\nand\n\n(ii) the disclosure is appropriate in all the circumstances.\n\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nForeign law enforcement  Division 4A\n\nSection 180B\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAuthorising access to prospective information or documents\nItem\nFor Attorney-General\nauthorisations under:\nthe investigation or proceeding is:\nsection 15D of the Mutual\nAssistance in Criminal Matters\nAct 1987\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto an offence against the law of a\nforeign country that:\n(a) is punishable by imprisonment for 3\nyears or more, imprisonment for\nlife or the death penalty; or\n(b) involves an act or omission that, if\nit had occurred in Australia, would\nbe a serious offence\nsection 78B of the International\nCriminal Court Act 2002\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a crime within the jurisdiction of the\nICC\nsection 34B of the International\nWar Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a War Crimes Tribunal offence\n\n(4) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police must revoke\nthe authorisation if he or she is satisfied that the disclosure is no\nlonger required.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of revocations.\n\n(5) An authorisation under subsection (2):\n\n(a) comes into force at the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation;\nand\n\n(b) ceases to be in force at the time specified in the authorisation,\nwhich must not be more than 21 days after the day the\nauthorisation is made, or that period as extended under\nsubsection (6), unless it is revoked earlier.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\nExtension of prospective authorisation\n\n(6) The period for which an authorisation under subsection (2) is in\nforce may be extended once only, by an authorised officer of the\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data",
      "skos:definition": "documents—enforcing international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2) of this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless:\n\n(a) the Attorney-General has authorised the making of the\nauthorisation under a provision mentioned in an item of the\nfollowing table; and\n\n(b) the authorised officer is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the disclosure is reasonably necessary for an\ninvestigation or proceeding referred to in that table item;\nand\n\n(ii) the disclosure is appropriate in all the circumstances.\n\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nForeign law enforcement  Division 4A\n\nSection 180B\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAuthorising access to prospective information or documents\nItem\nFor Attorney-General\nauthorisations under:\nthe investigation or proceeding is:\nsection 15D of the Mutual\nAssistance in Criminal Matters\nAct 1987\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto an offence against the law of a\nforeign country that:\n(a) is punishable by imprisonment for 3\nyears or more, imprisonment for\nlife or the death penalty; or\n(b) involves an act or omission that, if\nit had occurred in Australia, would\nbe a serious offence\nsection 78B of the International\nCriminal Court Act 2002\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a crime within the jurisdiction of the\nICC\nsection 34B of the International\nWar Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a War Crimes ",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979-sec-180b-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "86399a38a253c34ba8a8d9c824de8b9edc28e214898968d1635aef5c565534a0"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-act-1979#sec-180b",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "180B Authorisations for access to prospective information or",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 378,
      "values:originalText": "documents—enforcing international laws\nDisclosure to the Australian Federal Police\n\n(1) Sections 276, 277 and 278 of the Telecommunications Act 1997 do\nnot prevent a disclosure of information or a document if the\ninformation or document is covered by an authorisation in force\nunder subsection (2) of this section.\nProspective authorisation\n\n(2) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police may\nauthorise the disclosure of specified information or specified\ndocuments that come into existence during the period for which the\nauthorisation is in force.\n\n(3) The authorised officer must not make the authorisation unless:\n\n(a) the Attorney-General has authorised the making of the\nauthorisation under a provision mentioned in an item of the\nfollowing table; and\n\n(b) the authorised officer is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the disclosure is reasonably necessary for an\ninvestigation or proceeding referred to in that table item;\nand\n\n(ii) the disclosure is appropriate in all the circumstances.\n\n\nAccess to telecommunications data  Chapter 4\nPermitted access to telecommunications data  Part 4-1\nForeign law enforcement  Division 4A\n\nSection 180B\n\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAuthorising access to prospective information or documents\nItem\nFor Attorney-General\nauthorisations under:\nthe investigation or proceeding is:\nsection 15D of the Mutual\nAssistance in Criminal Matters\nAct 1987\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto an offence against the law of a\nforeign country that:\n(a) is punishable by imprisonment for 3\nyears or more, imprisonment for\nlife or the death penalty; or\n(b) involves an act or omission that, if\nit had occurred in Australia, would\nbe a serious offence\nsection 78B of the International\nCriminal Court Act 2002\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a crime within the jurisdiction of the\nICC\nsection 34B of the International\nWar Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nan investigation or proceeding relating\nto a War Crimes Tribunal offence\n\n(4) An authorised officer of the Australian Federal Police must revoke\nthe authorisation if he or she is satisfied that the disclosure is no\nlonger required.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of revocations.\n\n(5) An authorisation under subsection (2):\n\n(a) comes into force at the time the person from whom the\ndisclosure is sought receives notification of the authorisation;\nand\n\n(b) ceases to be in force at the time specified in the authorisation,\nwhich must not be more than 21 days after the day the\nauthorisation is made, or that period as extended under\nsubsection (6), unless it is revoked earlier.\nNote:\nSection 184 deals with notification of authorisations.\nExtension of prospective authorisation\n\n(6) The period for which an authorisation under subsection (2) is in\nforce may be extended once only, by an authorised officer of the\n\nChapter 4  Access to telecommunications data"
    },
